.TH CHMAP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_declare_scoped \- declare a hash map handle that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_declare_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, along with the companion type
variables that pass key_t and val_t on to every later type-inferred
macro, and gives the handle the cleanup attribute, which destroys the map
on every exit from the enclosing scope. Nothing is created, and the
handle holds NULL until one of the init macros runs.

Compared with chmap_declare(3), it sets the handle to NULL instead of
leaving it uninitialized, sets both companion type variables to NULL as
well, and gives the handle a cleanup attribute, so leaving the scope
destroys whatever map the handle names at that moment. Those are the
only differences. Since the handle starts as NULL and the library
ignores a NULL handle, returning before an init macro has run is safe,
and nothing in the block reads an indeterminate object.

Use it with chmap_init(3) or one of its variants when the configuration
of the map is decided inside the scope. chmap_construct_scoped(3) is the
one-step form for a map whose configuration is known at the
declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the init macro selects
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations and is not an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
An explicit chmap_destroy(3) before the end of the scope is fine. It
sets the handle to NULL, and the cleanup at scope exit then does
nothing
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it and do not store
it anywhere that outlives the scope, because either way you hand out a
pointer that is freed when the scope exits
The companion type variables live in the scope of the handle, so a
function that receives the handle needs chmap_redeclare(3) inside it
An array key or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
a key or a value by assignment and C cannot assign an array, so the map
would store the address of the caller's array instead of its elements. A
struct that holds the array works
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_declare_scoped(scores, char *, int);
if (!load_config()) {
    return -1;             /* nothing was created; cleanup is a no-op */
}
chmap_init(scores);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
return 0;                  /* scores is destroyed here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
